$DIR/main.p:19:2: cannot pass &x to hook (which may call (K).Keep), which keeps it after returning
    &x is a pointer to x. x is a local variable of f (declared at main.p:18).
    hook (which may call (K).Keep) stores p in package variable keep
    (main.p:12), so &x would outlive x.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If hook (which may call (K).Keep) should keep its own copy of x, change it
    to take the value, not a pointer, or an owner it can keep: an owned[int]
    from new, a shared[int], or a library owner such as a vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:26:2: cannot store k.Keep in package variable hook, which outlives init's frame
    A method value (at main.p:26) is a record in init's frame that holds its
    receiver, and calling it calls the method on that receiver. When init
    returns, its frame is reclaimed, but package variables live for the whole
    program, so package variable hook would be left pointing at reclaimed
    memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store the value itself instead of a view of init's frame, or allocate it so
    that it lives as long as package variable hook.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
